# Trivy findings accepted with justification.
#
# As with .checkov.yaml, each entry is a decision rather than an unfixed bug.

# AVD-DS-0026 - no HEALTHCHECK in the Dockerfile.
# Kubernetes owns liveness and readiness through the probes in the Helm chart.
# A second, differently-configured check inside the image would drift from those
# probes and make it ambiguous which check failed.
AVD-DS-0026

# AVD-KSV-0033 / AVD-KSV-0035 - "image must come from GCR / ECR".
# This platform runs on Azure and pulls from Azure Container Registry. The
# equivalent control is enforced elsewhere: the kubelet identity holds AcrPull on
# exactly one registry, and Azure Policy restricts allowed image sources.
AVD-KSV-0033
AVD-KSV-0035

# AVD-KSV-01010 - "ConfigMap stores sensitive content".
# Triggered by the key name LOG_LEVEL, whose value is a log level. Real secrets
# come from Key Vault through the CSI driver and never appear in a ConfigMap.
AVD-KSV-01010

# AVD-KSV-0039 / AVD-KSV-0040 - "namespace has no LimitRange / ResourceQuota".
# Both exist, in k8s/bootstrap/limit-ranges.yaml and resource-quotas.yaml. Trivy
# evaluates each document in isolation and cannot see the sibling file that
# satisfies the control for the same namespace.
AVD-KSV-0039
AVD-KSV-0040

# AVD-KSV-0048 - "Role grants write verbs on workload resources".
# That is exactly what the `operator` Role in k8s/bootstrap/rbac.yaml is for:
# restarting and scaling workloads during an incident. It deliberately excludes
# secrets, RBAC objects and any cluster-scoped resource, and the read-only
# `developer` Role is what most people get.
AVD-KSV-0048

# AVD-AZU-0041 - "AKS API server has no authorized IP ranges".
# The module default is empty, which is the dev posture. Staging and production
# both set api_server_authorized_ip_ranges (see their tfvars examples), and the
# variable exists precisely so the restriction is explicit per environment.
AVD-AZU-0041

# AVD-AZU-0047 - "security group rule allows ingress from any IP".
# The ingress subnet fronts a public HTTPS service, so 0.0.0.0/0 on 443 is the
# intended posture. It is governed by allowed_ingress_source_prefixes, which
# environments narrow to Front Door or corporate ranges when the service is not
# meant to be Internet-facing. No other subnet accepts traffic from the Internet.
AVD-AZU-0047

# AVD-AZU-0016 - "Key Vault has no purge protection" in dev and staging.
# Purge protection cannot be turned off once enabled, and it holds the vault
# name for the whole soft-delete window - which makes a destroy/recreate cycle
# impossible in an environment meant to be torn down. Production enables it
# (terraform/modules/platform/main.tf sets it from var.environment), and Trivy
# correctly reports the production environment as passing this check.
AVD-AZU-0016
